  ('flutter-startup-time', 'Flutter startup time: what actually moves the needle', 'A practical process for measuring cold start, cutting the main() budget, and avoiding the optimizations that do not survive a release build.', '2026-09-10', 'Performance', '["Flutter","Performance","DevTools"]', 4, 'Startup time is the first performance number users feel. It is also the one teams optimize last, because it is invisible in day-to-day development — your debug build is warm, your phone is fast, and the app is already open from the last time you tested.

Here is the process I use to find real wins, and the things that turned out not to matter.

## Measure the cold start, not the warm one

A cold start is the only number that reflects a new user opening your app from the store. On Android:

```bash
adb shell am force-stop com.example.app
adb shell am start -W -n com.example.app/.MainActivity
```

`-W` gives you `TotalTime` and `WaitTime`. Run it ten times on a mid-range physical device, not an emulator, and take the median. On iOS, use Instruments'' App Launch template or the Xcode organizer''s launch metrics for release builds.

Then trace it in DevTools. Attach the profiler and look at what happens between process start and the first frame: plugin initialization, `main()` work, the first widget build, image decoding, font loading, network calls that block the first screen.

If you skip this step, you will optimize the thing that looks slow in the code instead of the thing that is slow on the device.

## Treat main() as a budget

`main()` runs before anything is visible, and every millisecond there is a millisecond of blank screen. The rule: **only initialize what the first frame needs.**

- Call `WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized()` only if something before `runApp` needs bindings. Many apps call it out of habit.
- Do not `await` analytics, crash reporting, remote config, or ad SDKs before `runApp`. Start them after the first frame with `WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback`.
- Prefer lazy plugin initialization. Firebase, for example, can usually be initialized after the first frame unless the first screen depends on it.
- Move preference reads to a splash-level async gap, not a blocking await in `main`.

```dart
void main() {
  runApp(const App());

  WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback((_) async {
    await Firebase.initializeApp();
    await Analytics.start();
  });
}
```

If the first screen requires user state, show a deliberate skeleton instead of a blank launch screen. Perceived speed matters as much as measured speed.

## Defer what is not on screen one

Android deferred components let you split a Flutter library into a feature module downloaded on demand. For heavy flows — video players, editors, maps — this can meaningfully shrink the initial install and first launch. Two caveats: it is Android-only, and it adds build and QA complexity. Use it for genuinely large features, not for a settings screen.

For everything else, defer in Dart: lazy-load tabs, avoid constructing heavy controllers in the root widget, and do not parse large JSON payloads during the first build.

## Fonts and images are usually the quiet cost

Custom fonts are a common startup tax. Each weight is a file that must be loaded before text renders correctly.

- Subset fonts to the character sets you actually ship. A Gujarati-first app needs Gujarati glyphs; it does not need every Unicode block in the display font.
- Ship only the weights used on the first screen; load display weights for later screens lazily.
- Preload the one or two fonts needed for the first paint; let the rest swap in.
- For images, decode at display size with `cacheWidth`/`cacheHeight`. A 1200px image in a 120px thumbnail wastes memory and CPU on decode.
- Prefer WebP or AVIF for content images and verify with `flutter build --analyze-size`.

On one content-heavy app, resizing remote images at the CDN and decoding at display size did more for scroll smoothness than any widget refactor.

## Parse large JSON off the main isolate

If a screen waits for a large payload, parse it with `compute` so the UI thread stays free:

```dart
final loans = await compute(parseLoans, response.body);
```

For payloads under roughly 100 KB the overhead may not be worth it. Measure. The same goes for cryptography, image processing, and PDF generation — anything CPU-bound that runs while the user is looking at the screen.

## Build fewer widgets

The cheapest widget is the one you do not build. On first launch:

- Avoid `IndexedStack` with five heavy tabs that all build immediately. Use a lazy indexed stack or a `PageView` that constructs on demand.
- Do not put global listeners that rebuild the whole tree above the `MaterialApp`.
- Use `const` constructors where they are natural, but do not contort code for them. The compiler already handles most constant folding.
- Keep the first screen''s widget tree shallow. A hero section with twelve nested wrappers is twelve rebuild scopes.

## What did not help

- **Shader warmup.** With Impeller on iOS and modern Android, the old Skia shader compilation advice is mostly obsolete. Test before adding complexity.
- **Aggressive `const` everywhere.** Marginal. Readability wins.
- **Splash screens held longer** to "hide" slow startup. Users notice; it just moves the wait.
- **Removing the debug banner.** Obviously.

## A checklist I run before release

1. Cold start measured on a mid-range Android device and a two-year-old iPhone.
2. `main()` contains no awaited third-party initialization.
3. First frame does not wait on network.
4. Fonts subset and weights limited.
5. Images decoded at display size.
6. Large payload parsing moved off the UI isolate.
7. Tabs and heavy controllers constructed lazily.
8. Release build size inspected with `--analyze-size`.

Startup work is unglamorous, but it compounds: every session starts faster, on every device, forever. If your Flutter app feels slow before the first screen, [I can help you profile it](mailto:hasanabbassorathiya12@gmail.com).
', 1),
  ('localization-4-6-star-app', 'Localization lessons from a 4.6-star app', 'What building a Gujarati-first app with five languages taught me about scripts, RTL, plurals, content operations, and store listings.', '2026-08-27', 'Build in public', '["Localization","Flutter","Duas and Aamal"]', 4, 'Duas and Aamal ships in five languages — English, Urdu, Gujarati, Swahili, and Persian — with Arabic content at its core. It holds a 4.6-star rating, and the reviews that matter most are not about features. They are from people who finally have an app that speaks their language properly.

Localization is usually treated as a translation task at the end of a project. It is actually an architecture and product decision that starts on day one. These are the lessons I keep applying.

## Pick the language that gets forgotten

Every app translates English into the "big" languages first. For Duas and Aamal, the primary audience reads Gujarati, so Gujarati came first — not as a translation of an English interface, but as the language the interface was designed in.

That changes everything downstream:

- Buttons are sized for Gujarati string lengths, not English ones.
- The typography supports Gujarati script shaping and line height from the start.
- Content editors write in Gujarati, so the app sounds like the community, not like a translated product page.

If your product has a core audience whose language is an afterthought, flip the order. Design for them first; English will survive the adjustment.

## Script support is more than a font file

Arabic and Urdu are right-to-left, and Gujarati has its own shaping rules. A font that renders Latin perfectly can fail badly here.

What I check:

- **Shaping:** conjuncts and ligatures render correctly, not as disconnected glyphs.
- **Line height:** scripts with ascenders and descenders need more leading than Latin.
- **Font fallback:** a missing glyph should fall back to a font that has it, not show a tofu box.
- **Numerals:** decide whether to show Latin, Arabic-Indic, or Gujarati digits, and make it a user setting where it matters.

Test on the oldest supported OS version. Font fallback differences between iOS and Android are real and easy to miss on a new flagship.

## RTL is not a mirror

Flipping the whole layout with `Directionality` is a start, not a finish. These need explicit decisions:

- Icons that imply direction (back arrows, progress chevrons) should flip.
- Icons that represent objects (a clock, a book) should not.
- Numbers, phone numbers, and code stay left-to-right even inside an RTL layout.
- Charts and timelines need a deliberate reading direction.
- Text alignment inside forms should follow the field''s language, not the app''s locale.

The bug I see most often is a mirrored back arrow that now points forward while still navigating back. It looks minor until a user is lost.

## Plan for text expansion

German and Urdu strings can be 30% longer than English. A layout that only works in English will break the moment it is translated. Practical rules:

- Never fix the width of a button that contains a label.
- Let text wrap to two lines and design for it.
- Use `Flexible` and `softWrap` deliberately.
- Test with a pseudo-locale that adds padding characters to every string.

I add a pseudo-locale build to CI for exactly this reason. It catches truncation before a translator ever sees the app.

## Pluralization and dates are where translations go wrong

English has two plural forms. Arabic has six. Gujarati has its own rules. Hand-rolled string concatenation cannot express this.

Use ICU message format through `intl`:

```dart
final message = Intl.plural(
  count,
  zero: ''No duas saved'',
  one: ''One dua saved'',
  other: ''$count duas saved'',
  locale: localeName,
);
```

Dates are just as cultural. A prayer app needs both Hijri and Gregorian dates, local time zones, and prayer calculations that respect the user''s school of thought. "July 14" is not a universal representation of a day. I keep date formatting in one service with tests per locale, because scattered `DateFormat` calls become inconsistent fast.

## Content operations are part of localization

The app''s content changes weekly: new duas, corrections, seasonal material. If every content update requires an app release, the translation pipeline becomes a bottleneck.

We solved this with a small CMS (`duas-crm`) that stores content per language, tracks review status, and pushes updates to the app. The rules:

- Every content item has a language, a source, and a reviewer.
- Translations can ship independently of the binary.
- Nothing goes live without review by someone qualified in that language and subject.
- The app caches content for offline use and shows the last good version when offline.

This is not glamorous engineering, but it is why the app can respond to the community in days instead of months.

## Store metadata is localization too

The App Store and Play Store listings are the first screen most users see. Translate:

- App name and subtitle (within character limits).
- Screenshot captions, not just the screenshots.
- The description''s first two lines, which are what search results show.
- Keywords, researched per market rather than machine-translated.

A great app with an English-only listing is invisible to the audience that needs it most.

## The review that taught me the most

The most useful feedback was not a feature request. It was a one-star review from someone who could not find a setting because the label was translated literally and made no sense in their dialect. We fixed the wording and added a glossary so translators use consistent terms across the app.

Localization quality is not measured by the number of languages in the settings screen. It is measured by whether a user forgets they are using a translated app.

If you are building for a community whose language is underserved, [I would like to hear about it](mailto:hasanabbassorathiya12@gmail.com). Duas and Aamal is proof that this market rewards care.
', 1),
  ('from-shipping-apps-to-gde', 'From shipping apps to GDE: the work that actually compounds', 'I am not a Google Developer Expert yet. This is the deliberate 12-month plan I am running — and the parts of public work that actually count.', '2026-08-13', 'Career', '["Career","GDE","Community"]', 4, 'I am not a Google Developer Expert yet. I am building toward it deliberately, and I think the path is more concrete than most people admit.

A GDE is recognized by Google for expertise in a technology plus a track record of contributing to the developer community — talks, articles, open source, mentoring, and helping developers solve real problems. It is not a certification you buy, and it is not awarded for having a popular app. It is awarded for being useful in public, consistently.

Here is the flywheel I am running, and the parts that actually compound.

## 1. Ship first, then write

The strongest technical content comes from a problem you actually solved. Not "10 Flutter tips," but "why our checkout rebuilt 40 times a second and how we found it."

My rule: every article starts as a note from production. If I cannot point to the code, the error, or the decision, I do not write it. Readers can tell the difference immediately, and so can reviewers.

A practical format:

- **Problem:** what broke, with the symptom a user saw.
- **Investigation:** how I measured or narrowed it.
- **Fix:** the change, with the tradeoff I accepted.
- **Generalization:** the rule I now apply.

That structure produces content no tutorial can copy, because it is yours.

## 2. Publish on a cadence you can sustain

One excellent post every two weeks beats four rushed posts in a week followed by six months of silence. Consistency is the signal. It also compounds: search traffic, inbound messages, and talk invitations all arrive late and then accelerate.

My cadence:

- Two long-form posts per month, alternating Flutter engineering and AI-in-production topics.
- Short build notes in between — a screenshot, a benchmark, a bug that took a day.
- A monthly summary on LinkedIn that links to the canonical posts.

Everything lives on this site first. Cross-posting is distribution; the canonical link stays here.

## 3. Speak before you feel ready

The first talk does not need to be at a major conference. It needs to be useful to the people in the room.

The ladder I follow:

1. Internal engineering talk at work — low stakes, real audience.
2. Local Flutter or mobile meetup — 15 minutes, one lesson.
3. Community online sessions — broader reach, recorded.
4. Conference CFPs — submit six to get one, and expect silence.

A talk is not a blog post read aloud. The best ones show a problem live, fail once on purpose, and leave the audience with a checklist they can apply on Monday.

## 4. Maintain one thing in public

A pinned repository that is actively maintained signals more than ten abandoned experiments. My candidates are the ones where I have genuine domain depth:

- **stoolap-flutter** — embedded SQL for Flutter with vector search and reactive streams.
- **BULBVPN** — a Flutter VPN built for restricted networks.
- **wp-mcp-server** — an MCP server for the WordPress REST API.

The rule: issues answered, releases tagged, README that explains the why, and a path for a stranger to run it in five minutes. A small repo with a real user is worth more than a large repo with none.

## 5. Answer questions in public

The fastest way to find out what you do not understand is to answer a stranger''s question precisely. I spend thirty minutes a week on Flutter and Riverpod discussions, Stack Overflow, and GitHub issues.

Two rules:

- Answer the question asked, then add the context that prevents the next question.
- If I do not know, I say so and come back with what I found. Public honesty is a credential.

## 6. Build in public, not just ship in public

For Duas and Aamal and Lumio TV, I share the decisions — not the revenue, not vanity metrics, but the engineering choices: how localization works, why offline-first matters, what a 4.6-star review taught me. App users become readers; readers become users. The two audiences feed each other.

## What does not count

- **Buying visibility.** Followers do not make expertise.
- **One viral post.** A spike without a body of work is noise.
- **Surface-level tutorials.** "How to center a div in Flutter" is already answered ten thousand times.
- **Speaking only to sell.** The community can smell a pitch.

## A 12-month plan I can be held to

| Quarter | Focus | Output |
|---------|-------|--------|
| Q4 2026 | Foundation | 6 posts, 1 meetup talk, refresh 2 repos |
| Q1 2027 | Range | 6 posts, 2 CFPs submitted, 1 open-source release |
| Q2 2027 | Depth | Conference talk, 1 deep technical series, mentoring |
| Q3 2027 | Community | Workshop, collaboration with other Flutter devs |

If the work is consistent, the recognition follows. If it is not, no amount of networking fixes it.

## The honest scoreboard

I track what is measurable: posts published, talks given, issues answered, repos maintained, and inbound conversations that start with "I read your article." Not followers.

The goal is not the badge. The goal is to be the engineer other engineers can learn from. The badge is a side effect of doing that in public for long enough.

If you are on the same path, or you are hiring someone who is, [let''s compare notes](mailto:hasanabbassorathiya12@gmail.com). I am always up for a good conversation about Flutter, AI, and the unglamorous work of getting better.
', 1),
  ('leading-mobile-delivery-ev', 'Three operating rules from leading mobile delivery at an EV company', 'Release trains, a device matrix, and a written decision log — how mobile delivery changed shape when I stopped being the senior developer and became the lead.', '2026-07-30', 'Leadership', '["Leadership","Mobile","Delivery"]', 4, 'Leading mobile delivery is not a bigger version of being a senior developer. The job changes shape: your output is no longer the code you write, it is the decisions the team can make without you.

I led mobile engineering for EVLAB at Chalhoub Group, an electric mobility platform spanning rentals, leasing, and marketplace operations. These are the three operating rules that made the biggest difference.

## Rule 1: Run a release train, not a hero sprint

Every team says they release continuously. In practice, releases cluster around a deadline, then stall for weeks while a dozen branches wait for a QA window. The cost is not just delayed features — it is that integration risk keeps growing while nobody is shipping.

We moved to a release train:

- A release candidate is cut on a fixed day, every week or two.
- Anything not ready waits for the next train. No exceptions for "small" changes.
- The train leaves even if it carries a light load. The cadence matters more than the cargo.
- Hotfixes are the only bypass, and they require a written reason.

The discipline is in the "no exceptions" part. Once the train leaves on schedule three times in a row, teams start planning around it instead of negotiating with it. QA gets a predictable window. Stakeholders stop asking for dates and start asking what is on the train.

## Rule 2: Own the device matrix, not just the latest phone

Enterprise and consumer apps live on a long tail of devices: older Android versions, budget hardware, tablets, and in the EV case, vehicle-adjacent experiences. A build that is smooth on a flagship can be unusable on the phone a customer actually owns.

We kept an explicit matrix:

- The oldest OS version we support.
- The lowest-memory device we can buy.
- One tablet or large-screen configuration.
- One device with an unusual aspect ratio.

Every release candidate ran on all four before the train left. Not a full regression suite — a fifteen-minute smoke path: launch, sign in, browse, complete the primary task, check notifications.

This is unglamorous and it caught more real bugs than any automated suite we had. It also changed design conversations: when a designer proposes an interaction, someone can say "that animation drops frames on the low-memory device" before it is built.

## Rule 3: One decision log, written in public

The most expensive mobile decisions are the ones made twice. Why did we choose Riverpod over BLoC? Why is this screen native? Why does this API call happen on app start? Six months later, nobody remembers, and the team relitigates it with less context.

We kept a decision log in the repo:

```text
docs/decisions/
  0001-state-management.md
  0002-auth-token-storage.md
  0003-offline-cache-strategy.md
```

Each entry had: context, decision, alternatives considered, consequences, date, and owner. Not a design doc — a page at most.

The rule was that any change to architecture required an entry. This did three things:

1. It forced the decision to be explicit.
2. It gave new engineers a shortcut to the reasoning.
3. It made revisiting a decision a deliberate act, not an accident.

Writing the consequence section was the most valuable part. "This adds a dependency on Codemagic" or "this makes testing auth flows harder" is the information that actually shapes future work.

## The metrics that mattered

We tracked four numbers in the mobile operating review:

- **Crash-free sessions.** The single best proxy for whether users can do the thing.
- **Release lead time.** From merge to production. If it grows, process is winning over product.
- **Rollback rate.** A high number means quality gates are theatre.
- **First-response time on production issues.** Users forgive a bug that is acknowledged quickly.

Notice what is missing: story points, velocity, and lines of code. They measure activity, not delivery.

## What I would do differently

I would have started the decision log on day one instead of month three. I would also have involved QA earlier in the design of test plans for new features — by the time a feature reached QA, the test plan was usually a list of scenarios rather than a hypothesis about what could fail.

The last thing: protect the team''s focus. Every "quick sync" and "small change" has a context-switch cost that does not appear in any dashboard. The lead''s job is to absorb that cost so the engineers can stay in the work.

## The through-line

None of this is mobile-specific. Release trains, a real device matrix, and written decisions work for any delivery team. What makes mobile different is the surface area: two platforms, thousands of devices, store review, and users who update when they feel like it. You cannot test your way out of that. You can only build a system that makes the right behavior the default.

If you are scaling a mobile team or stepping into a lead role, [I am happy to talk through what worked](mailto:hasanabbassorathiya12@gmail.com) — including the parts that did not.
